\subsection*{3. Desugaring and Dunders}

Python for loops are syntactic sugar over the iter/next functions.
For example the for loop below

\begin{lstlisting}
for x in xs:
    foo()
\end{lstlisting}

gets converted into

\begin{lstlisting}
it = iter(xs)
try:
    while True:
        x = next(it)
        foo()
except StopIteration:
    pass
\end{lstlisting}

We can loop over the \lstinline{list}, \lstinline{range}, etc types because they implement the generator protocol.
See PEP255 \url{https://peps.python.org/pep-0255/} for details.

Any python object that implements the \lstinline{__iter__} dunder is called an \emph{iterable}.

